            <h2>21. Common Mistakes</h2>

            <h3>Mistake 1: Using section for every container</h3>

            <p>
                Not every visual container is a section. Use
                <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> when the content represents a meaningful
                thematic group.
            </p>

            <h3>Mistake 2: Missing a meaningful heading</h3>

            <p>
                Sections are easier to understand when their purpose is identified
                by an appropriate heading.
            </p>

            <h3>Mistake 3: Replacing every div with section</h3>

            <p>
                A <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> is still useful for generic grouping and
                styling when there is no specific semantic meaning.
            </p>

            <h2>22. Best Practices</h2>

            <ul>
                <li>Use sections for meaningful thematic groups.</li>
                <li>Give sections clear headings where appropriate.</li>
                <li>Use IDs when sections need direct navigation links.</li>
                <li>Nest sections only when the content hierarchy is meaningful.</li>
                <li>Use <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> when content is independently distributable.</li>
                <li>Use <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> for generic containers without semantic meaning.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>23. Practice Example</h2>

            <p>
                Create a page containing three sections:
                HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;main&gt;

    &lt;section id="html"&gt;

